WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.610
There’s a proverb that says behind every
great man is a great woman.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.950
That is certainly true of SpaceX.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.690
Elon Musk may be the most recognizable face
of his aerospace company.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.269
But his right-hand woman is Gwynne Shotwell.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:13.519
Hi, everybody.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:17.080
Shotwell is the President and Chief Operating
Officer of SpaceX.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.360
She manages the day-to-day operations and
the growth of the firm.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.420
In other words - she’s in charge of selling
rockets and dealing with Elon Musk.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.740
Unlike a lot of other SpaceX employees who
grew up fascinated by rockets, she wasn’t.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.640
When she was five and her neurosurgeon father
gathered the family around a TV to watch the

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.620
Apollo 11 moon landing, she found
it boring.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.420
She wasn’t interested in space at the time.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.219
She didn’t watch Star Trek as a kid.

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:43.530
Still, fate somehow put her on a path that
led to SpaceX.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.429
Shotwell grew up in Libertyville, Illinois,
a small town north of Chicago, the middle

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:49.450
child of two sisters.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:50.550
She had a mechanical mind.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.679
In the third grade, she asked
her mom how car engines worked.

00:00:53.679 --> 00:00:56.730
So her mother, an artist, bought her a book
about cars.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.269
In high school, she was a cheerleader and
played varsity basketball but wasn’t sure

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:02.379
what she wanted to do after graduating.

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:04.500
Her mum wanted Gwynne to be an engineer.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.170
And dragged her to a women’s engineering
event which would change the course of her life.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.550
While there, Shotwell admired the female mechanical
engineer on the panel and the beautiful suit

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:14.550
she wore.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.290
I loved her suit.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.070
Not a joke, I’m not exaggerating.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.840
The years have not changed this story.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.100
I asked her about her suit and what she did.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.100
And she owned her own company.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.240
She really inspired me.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.850
And that day, I said, ‘Okay, mom, you can
get off my back, I will be a mechanical engineer.’

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.100
Shotwell didn’t have to look far to find
a great school.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.200
A half hours’ drive from her hometown was
Northwestern University.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.290
It was the only school she applied to.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.770
She chose Northwestern because it was strong
in disciplines in addition to engineering

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.080
which suited her because she didn’t want
to be seen as a nerd .

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.710
Little did she know how cool geeks would become!

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:53.260
Shotwell was one of only three women out of
36 mechanical engineering undergrads but she

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.480
said she never had any issues being in what
was still largely considered a ‘man’s world'.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.740
She did have a problem landing an internship
one summer at a heating, ventilation and air

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.430
conditioning design company.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.720
The boss was impressed with her resume but
when he spoke to her over the phone for the

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.329
first time, this is how she said the conversation
went as published in Northwestern University’s

00:02:11.329 --> 00:02:12.329
magazine.

00:02:12.329 --> 00:02:14.390
“The guy said, ‘You’re a girl?’

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:15.849
I said, ‘Well, of course, I’m a girl.’

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:17.720
He says, ‘Well, you can’t possibly do
this job.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.219
There’ll be heavy lifting.’

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:20.430
I said, ‘I lift weights.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.349
I’m an athlete,’ and he basically said,
‘You can’t have this job’ and hung up.

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:26.819
It was so blatant — what a jerk.”

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:31.290
She graduated with a Bachelor of Science in
Mechanical Engineering in 1986 and interviewed

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.499
with IBM on the day of the Space Shuttle Challenger
disaster – a mission which had garnered

00:02:35.499 --> 00:02:38.069
a lot of attention because a teacher was on
board.

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:39.900
The tragedy shook Shotwell.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.870
She didn’t get the job at IBM but she was
hired by Chrysler as part of a program that

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.849
groomed graduates for management.

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:49.769
She did everything at Chrysler from rebuilding
engines to working alongside engineers who

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.260
designed the cars.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.579
But she didn’t find the work interesting
so she left and went to grad school, also

00:02:54.579 --> 00:02:56.079
at Northwestern.

00:02:56.079 --> 00:02:59.939
After getting her Master’s in Applied Mathematics,
she ran into a former teaching assistant who

00:02:59.939 --> 00:03:03.629
happened to be working at The Aerospace Corporation
in California.

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:07.750
He suggested she do the same, so she applied
and was hired as a thermal analyst, which

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:12.090
included running models on supercomputers
of the space shuttle heating in real-time

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.189
and feeding that information to NASA.

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:15.749
But this work didn’t excite her either.

00:03:15.749 --> 00:03:18.269
She was itching to go out and create something.

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:22.209
After a decade at The Aerospace Corporation,
she moved over to Microcosm, a low-budget

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:26.329
rocket builder, where she became a director,
focused on selling services to the government

00:03:26.329 --> 00:03:27.599
and other firms.

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:31.779
She honed the business skills that would later
be crucial to the success of SpaceX.

00:03:31.779 --> 00:03:34.370
How did she land at SpaceX?

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:39.220
According to the book Liftoff by Eric Berger,
a colleague of Shotwell’s at Microcosm,

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:44.280
engineer Hans Koenigsmann, was about to leave
to join SpaceX which was then a little-known startup.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.870
Shotwell took him out for a going-away lunch
and then dropped him off at his new digs.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.359
Koenigsmann offered to show her around.

00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:52.060
And that’s when Shotwell met Musk.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.329
Musk talked about his ideas for cutting the
cost of space travel by building his own rockets in-house.

00:03:57.329 --> 00:04:00.590
Shotwell was impressed by his ambition
to shake up the aerospace industry.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:03.519
She, too, was frustrated by the lack of innovation.

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:07.110
She mentioned that he should hire someone
dedicated to sales to find a customer for

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.529
SpaceX’s first rocket, the Falcon 1.

00:04:09.529 --> 00:04:14.267
Musk took her advice to heart and later offered
her the job, though she didn’t say yes right away.

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:18.680
She already had a stable job, two young children
to look after, and only just divorced her

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.889
first husband.

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:24.240
When she gave a virtual commencement address
at her alma mater in 2021, she elaborated

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.271
on her difficult decision.

00:04:25.271 --> 00:04:30.050
It seemed so risky for me personally to join
this little start-up, in an industry where

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.980
none had ever succeeded.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:37.030
At the time I was a part-time single mother,
and this was just too far out of my comfort zone.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.870
I was driving on the freeway here in L.A.
when it finally hit me: I was being a total idiot.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:46.389
Who cares if I tried this job and either I
failed or the company failed?

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:51.259
What I recognized at that moment was that
it was the trying part that was the most important.

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:54.129
Try that risky thing, be a part of something
exciting.

00:04:54.129 --> 00:04:58.120
I don’t want to imagine what my life and
career would be like had I said no.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.530
I’m sure I would have been fine but I would
not have been a part of this amazing company,

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.590
working alongside such extraordinary people.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:07.770
Not taking that job would have been the fail.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:11.520
Shotwell joined SpaceX as the Vice-President
of Sales in 2002.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.050
She was employee number 11.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.300
There’s Gwynne at her cube.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.590
Today - some call her the “secret sauce”
behind the company’s success because of

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.729
her engineering expertise, business know-how,
and connections in the industry which helped

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:25.919
her land lucrative contracts.

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:31.009
After SpaceX launched the Falcon 1 into orbit
on its fourth attempt in 2008, she negotiated

00:05:31.009 --> 00:05:36.730
a $1.6 billion contract with NASA to deliver
cargo and supplies to astronauts at the International

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:38.080
Space Station.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.000
That deal saved SpaceX from bankruptcy.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.470
And in 2013, she was reportedly instrumental
in securing a 20-year lease of launchpad 39A

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.569
at the Kennedy Space Center, the historic
launchpad previously used by the Apollo moon

00:05:50.569 --> 00:05:52.790
missions and space shuttle flights.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.729
Despite her success at the right hand of Musk,
it’s not easy working for him as some former

00:05:56.729 --> 00:05:59.619
employees at his other company, Tesla, have
attested.

00:05:59.619 --> 00:06:01.599
An ex-manager there told Business Insider:

00:06:01.599 --> 00:06:05.410
"It's a culture in which, if you don't have
a solution to a problem and you don't have

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:09.129
that problem resolved within a few days or
a week or two, you're gone.

00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:11.180
So it's better to keep your mouth shut."

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.220
On the other hand, Shotwell believes Musk
actually drives you to do your best work,

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:16.280
saying at a TED Conference

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.909
"...when Elon says something, you have to
pause and not immediately blurt out, 'Well,

00:06:20.909 --> 00:06:22.860
that's impossible’ or ‘There's no way
we're gonna do that...'

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:26.707
So you zip it, think about it, and you find
ways to get that done.”

00:06:26.707 --> 00:06:29.099
God speed Bob and Doug.

00:06:29.099 --> 00:06:34.234
SpaceX was the first private company to transport
American astronauts to the ISS.

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:38.189
Next, it plans to take humans to the moon
and later to Mars on a fully reusable rocket

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:39.410
named Starship.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.810
We want to make the world a better place.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:46.688
And our contribution there is to develop systems
that will take people to other planets,

00:06:46.688 --> 00:06:48.599
kind of proliferate the human species.

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:51.599
And I know that sounds crazy, doesn’t sound very
business-y,

00:06:51.599 --> 00:06:53.434
but frankly that’s why we’re in business.

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:59.060
Over the past two decades, Shotwell has helped
grow SpaceX from 10 employees to 10,000.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.110
From zero revenue to billions of dollars annually.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:04.349
Musk and Shotwell may appear to be polar opposites.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:06.169
He can be awkward at times.

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:07.630
She’s smooth and all smiles.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.599
Yet they have a shared ambition: to get things
done.

00:07:11.599 --> 00:07:15.300
If you’ve been inspired by Shotwell’s
story and want to follow in her footsteps,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.490
a solid background in engineering and mathematics
is crucial.

